WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.300
Last clip a publish was bound
to the exact payload you read.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.100
Now money gets the same treatment.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.633
Your ad budget is steered by what a
customer really costs and returns,

00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:11.433
and no money moves until a
different person says yes.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.233
In this clip you'll sync spend,
set a target, read pacing,

00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:18.400
and approve a budget move.
Performance ads proposes.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.333
It never moves money on its own.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:22.800
Leo's ad platforms optimize
for their own results.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.333
Finance asks what a customer really cost,

00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:27.300
and the answer takes a
spreadsheet and a week.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.033
Meanwhile a budget keeps running on
a campaign that stopped paying back.

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:34.000
First, spend. It syncs
from Google and Meta,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.233
and arrives read only from other
platforms through Composio.

00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:40.400
In the console: Engines,
Performance ads, Platforms.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.467
Re-syncing the same window
updates the rows already there,

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:46.200
so spend and conversions
never count twice.

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:50.400
Second, what good looks like. You write
it in a unit economics definition:

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.567
a target cost to acquire a
customer, or a target return,

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.967
or a maximum payback in months, with
gross margin and revenue per customer.

00:00:56.967 --> 00:01:00.900
From those it sets a spend ceiling
and a first order profit gate.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.500
It's a definition like any other,
so a different person signs it.

00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:05.733
Now read a campaign.

00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:09.667
In the console: Engines, Performance
ads, Campaigns, then open one.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:14.367
You'll see spend, conversions, pacing
and any open moves in one place.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:17.967
The figures are the synced ones,
so when a number looks wrong,

00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:20.367
check the sync before
you blame the campaign.

00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:24.133
Pacing compares what you spend
with what your CAC target allows.

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:28.033
In the console: the Pacing tab,
with MER and CAC payback tiles.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:32.000
Real CAC and return come from paid
visits credited by campaign tag.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.833
Or ask your agent: "Where
should budget go this week? "

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:39.967
Now the gotcha. A paid visit is credited
to a campaign by its campaign tag,

00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:41.633
so put one on every ad link.

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:44.900
Without it, GTM OS can't tie
the visit to the campaign.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.900
The tile shows a dash with
the reason, never a guess.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.233
Ask your agent: "Which campaigns
are missing a campaign tag?

00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:53.200
" Then fix the links at the source.

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:57.567
Third, the allocator. Each night it
reads pacing and proposes budget moves.

00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:00.533
It only proposes. Every move
needs a different approver,

00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:02.667
and one move can at most double a budget.

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:06.467
In Dry Mode the plan is a
draft nobody can execute.

00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:09.867
In the console: Engines,
Performance ads, Open moves.

00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:12.600
Or ask your agent: "Show
the last allocator run".

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:14.533
Real money sits behind an approval.

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:17.300
Changing a budget or pausing
a campaign is a proposal.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.933
Sophia opens the approval, reads
the frozen change and its impact,

00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:22.300
and approves or rejects.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.733
Neither the proposer nor the human
the agent acts for can approve.

00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:28.767
An approved change can be undone,
through an approval as well.

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:32.433
Ask your agent: "Propose pausing
the Brand Search campaign".

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.233
Caps stand around all of it. In
the console: Settings, Safety.

00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:40.200
A guardrail limits ad spend per day,
and above it a person has to approve.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.700
Cost budgets in the ledger turn a breach

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.500
into an approval instead
of a silent overrun.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.833
Operator tip: stay in Dry for a
few weeks, read each night's plan,

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:50.667
and move nothing until you trust it.

00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:55.100
Or ask your agent: "How much did we
spend yesterday against the cap? "

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:56.833
It improves with cleaner inputs.

00:02:56.833 --> 00:03:00.000
Each night's plan reads fresher
spend and better attribution,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.267
so tag your campaigns and
keep the sync running.

00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:06.167
To customize, set your targets and
margin, the campaigns in scope,

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:07.100
and the spend cap.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.767
Each is a definition, so a
different person signs every change.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.033
Performance ads reads revenue
truth and attribution from Money.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:16.600
It answers to the ad
spend guardrail in Safety,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.033
and it feeds the spend
tile on Mission Control.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.600
Not in the product yet:
the ads creative loop,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.233
so this engine doesn't
generate or rank ad creative.

00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:27.167
Audience sync and offline conversion
upload aren't built either.

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:29.767
Spend from LinkedIn and
TikTok is read only.

00:03:29.833 --> 00:03:30.833
The skeptic's line:

00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:33.433
an allocator that reads
my numbers every night

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:35.500
will drain a budget before I wake up.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.467
It can't. It only proposes, one
move can at most double a budget,

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:40.667
and every move waits for
a different approver.

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:43.067
In Dry Mode it can't even be executed.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.000
In one pass: Spend syncs
without double counting,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.567
pacing compares it with your
target, and the allocator proposes.

00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:52.733
The engine proposes the move.
A person moves the money.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.933
Spend follows the payback,
every move waits for a person,

00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:58.133
and a re-sync never inflates your numbers.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.800
Tag one campaign today and
watch its tile fill in.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.333
Next is Research agent, a
researcher on every account.
